WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.444
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.892 --> 00:00:07.350
PRASHANTH SANAGAVARAPU:
Today we're

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.780
going to talk about
how we are optimizing

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.410
our SRE 's effectiveness,
enabling our engineering

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:18.510
teams to deliver applications in
a reliable, stable performance.

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.880
And providing digital
experience that

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:24.820
matches our first
in class journalism.

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:27.340
Agenda is to go or
some of the challenges

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.550
that we have in our
environment, and talk

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.880
about how we work as a team.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.520
And then Vinessa will
unpack some of the ways

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:42.050
how we are building operational
maturity across the teams using

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.330
product mindset.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:47.540
And then I'll walk you
into an engagement model

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.690
that we use to work with
the engineering teams

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:56.110
and develop tooling
or lessons that could

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:59.350
be applied across the arc.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:03.280
And then we'll also take
you through some of the ways

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.270
that we evangelize
our SRE practices

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.600
that are meant to drive the
culture change that you'd

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.860
need when you have your
applications running

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.282
in the cloud.

00:01:16.282 --> 00:01:19.900
Firstly, the challenges.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:26.230
In 2018 we transformed
our infrastructure.

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:30.430
We shut down our data centers
and migrated to the cloud.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.280
This transformation was
not just about migration.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:38.290
But the tooling that we used
to have and the culture we

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:42.690
had around how we manage
our infrastructure.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:49.500
We adopted
contemporization and put

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.910
in tools that would enable us
to build in a cloud native way.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:58.610
I'll talk more into that
tooling, but during that time

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.710
we built the
tooling that enabled

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:04.580
our engineers, basically, to
deliver applications faster.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:07.910
And the migration was
successful and out on time.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:10.620
This all sounds like
happily ever after.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.050
But right after
the migration, we

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.620
realized our real SRE
work has just begun.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:24.130
Let's take a look at one
of our business workflow

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.695
that we have that
spans across the teams.

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.320
The workflow is
basically newsroom

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.580
trying to publish an
article and it shows up

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.920
on our front end applications.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.830
The editor would compile
the article in our content

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.310
management system, hit Publish.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.800
The article lands on
our publishing pipeline.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.990
And the content APIs
formats this content

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:59.720
into a way that is consumable
by all the front end apps.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.320
At a high level, this
is a simple workflow.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.720
But during the migration,
what we did was

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.540
we enabled the teams
by providing autonomy

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:12.020
over their cloud
infrastructure by giving them

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.220
full control of their
infrastructure in their GCP

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:17.520
projects.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.106
So if you were to look at the
content management system here.

00:03:22.106 --> 00:03:26.010
It is made of
microservices, but the team

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.460
that manages that
system owns end-to-end

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.910
of all the microservices on
[INAUDIBLE] in their own GCP

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:33.630
project.

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:38.910
And so are the other
systems in this workflow.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:42.790
The challenge here is
our user experience

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:46.660
depends on all these systems
that we are seeing here.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.850
We did not have
an aggregated view

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:57.560
of how we measure our
user experience when teams

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.530
are working in their
own GCP projects

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.370
and working with their own
infrastructure in a way

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:10.030
that they're missing out on
how their system is impacting

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:14.080
a wider user experience.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.540
From an engineering standpoint,
there are two users here.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:23.040
One, the newsroom that's
trying to publish an article.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:27.300
And, two, the readers
that are consuming.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.990
So we had to rethink
and we saw a need

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:37.000
for building those aggregated
views of measuring how our user

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.180
experience gets impacted
when these systems are making

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:41.680
changes.

00:04:46.907 --> 00:04:48.490
So initially I spoke
about the tooling

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:51.550
that we built that
enabled the migration.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.860
Basically what we
did was we adapted

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.650
to the cloud native tools.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:01.030
We have consistency
ICD withdrawn,

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:04.360
and we started using
Kubernetes areas,

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:08.960
and ask Google to manage our
Kubernetes, which is GKE.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.150
We deployed NewYorkTimes.com
on GKE a year ago.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.760
And we have Prometheus
for understanding

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.380
how the systems perform.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:23.270
We also have Fastly
available self-serviceable,

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:29.560
distributed
edge-caching tool that

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.710
is basically available for
any front end engineering team

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:34.460
to put their application on.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:40.480
And we also have transformed
from being this data

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:43.600
center based infrastructure
to infrastructure S

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:44.760
code with Terraform.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:48.640
And we also have [INAUDIBLE]
to support the applications'

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.870
needs like secrets
management with Vault and PKI

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:59.010
management with Vault. So
the tooling is already there.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:02.060
But what we are
missing is the company

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.010
that you don't
see on this slide.

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:05.471
That is the application.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.470
So we have the
tooling, but what we

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:14.290
need is operational
maturity on the teams

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:19.660
to use these tools in a way
that they can build applications

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:22.030
that match their silos, that
match the user experience

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:26.480
that is required from them.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:31.930
So having that
operational maturity

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:36.160
alongside of these great tools
is a need that we have seen.

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:43.190
So what is the lay of the
land at the New York Times?

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.540
We have 400 plus applications.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:50.460
And we have 40 plus teams
managing 400 applications.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:55.100
And these applications
range from cloud native,

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:58.850
user facing apps to
enterprise apps that

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:00.740
are built by third
party but the teams

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:03.360
deploy for our internal use.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.385
So there is a wide
variety of these apps.

00:07:06.385 --> 00:07:07.760
With those wide
variety there are

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:11.820
wide variety of needs for
these apps to be in the cloud.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.450
That reflects in the teams, too.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:22.080
The teams have different
experience and skills,

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.350
especially around
SRE skills that they

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:28.910
would need to manage depending
on the type of the app.

00:07:28.910 --> 00:07:32.560
However, what is consistent
is after the migration

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.130
we were asking the
teams to manage

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:35.540
their own infrastructure.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:38.630
That is consistent,
regardless of what type of app

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:43.430
they use or build.

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:48.760
So what we saw is we as a team
had to work with these teams.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:53.500
Engage with them and build
skills on their teams

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.720
that would enable them
regardless of what type of app

00:07:55.720 --> 00:08:01.360
they're managing would
be successful in meeting

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:02.100
their SLOs.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:13.830
So in the data center world,
we had a centralized ops team.

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:17.140
So we came from that
world to the cloud.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:19.990
And this centralized ops team
was managing infrastructure

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:20.890
for the teams.

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:25.090
So that is a culture
change that we've

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:27.240
gone through in the last
one and a half years.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:32.080
But, however, the processes
that we have still

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:34.059
reflect that data center model.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:38.830
And we still
continue to use them,

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:42.780
which is more like trying to
fit a square peg in round hole.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:48.280
I'll give you an example.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.040
Our biggest example
that I can think

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:52.930
of when it comes to managing
systems in the data center way

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:58.800
is our priority one
defect management process.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.580
Where it works really well if
we apply it to a single team

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:05.250
because that's what
it is built for,

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:09.750
but when an audit spans
across multiple teams, then

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.010
it becomes really hard.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:15.210
The teams try to
figure out the problem

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.740
and then pass that
problem to other teams,

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:21.670
figuring out this is not
the cause that we have.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:24.270
And it goes to the other
team and goes to another team

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:27.840
until finally, a
resolution is arrived.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:30.300
So we've seen that
process fail when

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:34.080
we have distributed
engineering teams managing

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.160
their own infrastructure.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:40.550
And we had to rethink the
way how we could manage

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:42.380
our priority one defects.

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:45.030
So that's just an example.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:47.480
So we had to rethink
about the processes

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:53.230
that we have from
the data center.

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:56.632
How did we address
all these problems?

00:09:56.632 --> 00:09:57.340
You can guess it.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:00.290
Yes, through SRE program.

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.170
But how did we do it?

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:10.450
For most of our
teams, when we talk

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:12.970
about how we formed the team
there is always a back story.

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:17.890
And who is an SRE is an
interesting conversation

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:20.620
that I often hear.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:25.180
But our team is pretty much
what the book prescribes.

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:27.980
We went by the book.

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:33.440
We built tooling and automation
to help our engineering teams

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:35.870
to manage their
production systems.

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:41.370
May be incident management,
may be tracking their SLOs.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:45.820
The most important
thing that we do

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:48.610
is engage with the
engineering teams.

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:51.880
You engage to build the
operational maturity

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.180
on the teams.

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:57.190
It could be on a
specific problem,

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:01.040
or it could be a
tooling app that

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:04.290
might be useful for
other engineering teams.

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:06.660
Or it could just
be anything that

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:10.160
involves building
reliability into the systems.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:14.980
That's what we engage on
a one on one basis for.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:17.710
We also provide
documentation on process

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:22.240
as a way to guide these
teams to use our tooling,

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:24.870
to evangelize the SRE practices.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:29.770
And we go one step further
and provide workshops.

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:33.810
Workshops around how to
be an incident commander.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:39.793
Workshops around how do
you manage your incidents?

00:11:39.793 --> 00:11:41.710
So that's the process
and guidance we provide.

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:46.800
So now you've seen
the challenges.

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:49.560
You've see what we do as a team.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:51.060
Now my colleague,
Vinessa, will talk

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:55.040
about how the program works to
build that operational maturity

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:56.040
I've been talking about.

00:12:00.067 --> 00:12:00.900
VINESSA WAN: Thanks.

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:03.760
Thanks, Prashanth.

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:06.430
So, Product Management mindset.

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:09.760
So, Prashanth mentioned
we had a central team that

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:12.970
controlled all of the
infrastructure decisions

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:15.830
for New York Times applications.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:21.100
So shifting to GCP and
making that decision

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:23.740
to have now application
development teams manage

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:27.400
their own infrastructure
required a shift

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:29.420
in our thinking.

00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:31.960
Our team delivery engineering
with the SRE function

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:34.810
was formed not to control
the infrastructure.

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:38.500
Because other app teams control
their own infrastructure.

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:41.590
We sought to
empower these teams.

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:44.310
So therefore we had to shift
our way of thinking as well.

00:12:47.843 --> 00:12:50.010
I know, you get a project
manager for the first time

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:51.385
at a tech conference
you're going

00:12:51.385 --> 00:12:52.680
to hear the Agile Manifesto.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:56.250
But that was a source of
inspiration for how we

00:12:56.250 --> 00:13:00.290
decided to operationalize SRE.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:03.995
Individuals and interactions
over processes and tools.

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:10.230
And it's not to say that the
tooling and processes and all

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:11.890
the cool things
that you see today,

00:13:11.890 --> 00:13:14.050
and all the great
things that we do--

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:18.080
I mean, they're vital
to making change.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:20.420
But what we're
talking about is when

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:23.330
you are aiming that teams
have to be operationally

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:24.800
mature, which
means they're going

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:29.630
to be managing SRE
practices on their own,

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:31.300
that's a culture change.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:32.470
And that starts with people.

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:37.760
Product management
thinking starts

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:42.770
with viewing our internal
teams as customers.

00:13:42.770 --> 00:13:46.340
And what that means is
you have to incentivize

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:48.770
people using teams,
using your tooling,

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:50.990
teams listening to
your guidance, teams

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:54.480
using and applying
your processes.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.560
I'm going to be honest.

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:58.730
This sometimes is the
hardest part of my job.

00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:00.680
It would be a lot
easier in the short term

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:05.600
if we just require
teams to use our tools.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:09.800
But that comes at that
risk because of what

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:11.720
it means if we don't
do this, we risk

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:14.060
not being able to provide
tooling that speeds up

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:16.040
development, that
improves velocity,

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:22.440
that helps teams deliver a
reliable end user experience.

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:25.040
So we, ourselves,
are incentivized

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:28.640
to constantly seek that feedback
and get that data to drive

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:29.975
our decision making process.

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:36.790
Our team formed in March
2016 and we called out

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:40.270
and identified a focus of
how we were going to collect

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:41.380
this feedback and data.

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:46.630
It was going to be around
how teams perceived us.

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:49.240
Why did we need to
measure perception?

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:53.660
Our old infrastructure
and systems teams,

00:14:53.660 --> 00:14:56.180
they were perceived as
blockers and bottlenecks

00:14:56.180 --> 00:14:58.760
because they had to
control everything

00:14:58.760 --> 00:15:01.340
as part of the mission and
the way we were structured.

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:04.320
And so we wanted to change
that perception as well.

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.100
We wanted to build
trust with teams

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:09.530
to feel that they can rely
on us, on our tooling,

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:11.980
and our best practices.

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:14.960
So the feedback
process has evolved.

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:17.370
We've been running the
process for a couple of years.

00:15:17.370 --> 00:15:18.990
And it's evolved a bit.

00:15:18.990 --> 00:15:21.590
But we've come up with
a really stable process.

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:24.650
And it's very much
modeled what you might

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:26.750
see from front end design.

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:29.150
And the process runs
about twice a year.

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:32.630
And the process, it builds
on user empathy as well as

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:37.660
the ability to measure
patterns over time.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:40.090
We always aim for
balanced representation

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:43.252
because we're building
tools for all of the Times.

00:15:43.252 --> 00:15:44.960
And when I say a
balanced representation,

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:47.410
it's not just
hearing from teams,

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:48.940
but recently, we've
also been aiming

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:51.610
to make sure the
non-engineers in the team

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:53.770
understand the value we provide.

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:56.590
Because a lot of times, it's
those folks that actually

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:57.895
control and govern the roadmap.

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:05.330
And then of course it
has to help us plan.

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:07.450
It has to be actionable.

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:09.840
And it's not to say that this
is the only source of input

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:10.680
into our roadmap.

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:14.530
There's always top down
efforts that have to be done,

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:17.640
particularly in the security
and compliance arena

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.480
but this is a major input
that fuels what we do

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:22.740
and where our focus will be.

00:16:28.032 --> 00:16:29.740
The way we collect
feedback is two parts.

00:16:29.740 --> 00:16:32.460
It's a survey and it's
qualitative interviews.

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:34.620
The survey itself is
very much designed

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:36.030
with user empathy in mind.

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:37.620
It's very short.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:39.840
It's mostly
quantitative questions.

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:42.060
Again, we want to make
it easy for people.

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:44.910
If you make something easy
in terms of getting feedback,

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:47.787
you're going to
get more feedback.

00:16:47.787 --> 00:16:48.620
It's obvious, right?

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:50.720
But the fact of the
matter is sometimes

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:53.340
people want to give
a lot of information,

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:58.587
but you can't substitute a
survey with a conversation.

00:16:58.587 --> 00:17:00.920
So what we do, and this is
one of my favorite questions.

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:02.950
Our very first question--

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:04.490
let me see if I
can use a pointer--

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:07.069
is we say we value
your feedback.

00:17:07.069 --> 00:17:09.710
If you want to talk with
us, leave your name here

00:17:09.710 --> 00:17:12.109
and we'll reach out and have
a conversation with you.

00:17:12.109 --> 00:17:14.599
And we handle that as part
of the qualitative interview

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:16.089
process.

00:17:16.089 --> 00:17:18.950
Now the process actually
started with just a few managers

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:21.180
on the team doing
these interviews.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:24.349
But we decided to actually
open this whole process up so

00:17:24.349 --> 00:17:28.310
that anyone in our
team, any engineer

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:30.590
can sign up to interview
someone or even

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:33.040
scribe for an interview.

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:35.350
This has delivered
benefits, not just in

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:38.320
terms of us being
able to get better

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:41.750
reach in getting more feedback.

00:17:41.750 --> 00:17:44.460
It also builds a
lot more empathy.

00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:46.422
When you have a
conversation with people,

00:17:46.422 --> 00:17:47.880
they feel like
they're being heard,

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:50.760
but it also motivates
our engineers.

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:52.710
I mean, how often in an
internal tooling team

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:56.560
do you actually get to
do a practice like this?

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:58.380
And this is a catalyst
also because it

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:01.200
let us create much better
guidelines and templates

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:04.440
and how we actually create
qualitative interviews.

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:08.220
And so by having this we're able
to collect all this information

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:11.590
in a consistent way.

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:13.870
We take these results
and we synthesize them

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:15.820
as a team to make
sure that we have

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:17.945
a correct view on our findings.

00:18:17.945 --> 00:18:19.570
This means if you're
in test automation

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:23.860
or even if you're in SRE,
you are part of this process.

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:25.420
And you're represented.

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:29.290
And then we carve out a
whole week where we dive

00:18:29.290 --> 00:18:32.330
into-- and we call it the
team brainstorm week--

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:35.200
and this is when we go
through all the pain points

00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:36.827
that people went through.

00:18:36.827 --> 00:18:39.160
What are things that we're
doing that we should actually

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:41.260
continue or do more of?

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:42.940
And what are the
opportunities that we

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:46.730
need to explore as
part of our roadmap?

00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:49.450
And then of course we
share these results out.

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:51.605
It's very important, I
want to emphasize this,

00:18:51.605 --> 00:18:53.230
because sometimes
there's this tendency

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:56.180
to collect this information
and it could be really hard.

00:18:56.180 --> 00:18:58.180
It's hard to sometimes
say, yeah we really

00:18:58.180 --> 00:18:59.740
didn't do really great at it.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:02.920
But it's more important
to say, we recognize this

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:04.930
and this is what
we're doing about it.

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:08.690
Or what we even heard from
the feedback is, hey I get it.

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:10.540
If you can't do
everything because there's

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:11.900
just so much to do.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:14.050
But at least can you tell
me that you're hearing it

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:16.163
and understand and explain
what is the other--

00:19:16.163 --> 00:19:17.080
I see someone nodding.

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:19.630
You get it.

00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:21.750
So we share this feedback
for the coming quarters

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:24.990
and then we say, OK, here
are potential projects,

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:27.120
but it's also a
filter in terms of who

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:30.420
are teams that we should be
partnering with to work out

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:31.618
these complex problems.

00:19:31.618 --> 00:19:33.660
And that is what Prashanth
is going to talk about

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:34.723
in the engagement model.

00:19:34.723 --> 00:19:36.140
PRASHANTH SANAGAVARAPU:
Thank You.

00:19:40.560 --> 00:19:42.540
Engagements are
opportunities for your team

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:46.320
to work with other engineering
teams on a one on one basis

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:50.550
and focus on maximizing
the results of why

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:53.490
you are engaging with.

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:56.160
The idea here is to
generate automation,

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:59.880
or learning that could be
applied across the arc.

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:06.710
And we work with the
engineering teams

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:09.500
and we focus on maximizing
the results by following

00:20:09.500 --> 00:20:10.520
our engagement model.

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:18.530
So if you are an SRE
engineering manager,

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:21.990
it's very important to
define who an SRE is,

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:23.960
and what his role
is or her role is.

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:29.430
And manage communications
and collaborations

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:34.140
across the team to build
that operational maturity.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:37.320
But for that you have
to proactively define

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:38.970
all of that in your
engagement model.

00:20:41.917 --> 00:20:44.000
You may ask why would we
need an engagement model.

00:20:46.683 --> 00:20:48.350
When we did not have
an engagement model

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:53.330
we were still working with
teams and engaging with them.

00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:56.050
However, the issue
there was we always

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:58.510
had misaligned priorities.

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:04.790
And we were in a situation
in one of our engagements

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:07.430
where whether focus
on capacity planning

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:09.830
or whether we want to
build on tooling app

00:21:09.830 --> 00:21:15.500
that is like a blue-green
deployments on our Kubernetes

00:21:15.500 --> 00:21:19.690
tooling that we provide
to our engineering teams.

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:21.310
So not just the
priority alignment.

00:21:21.310 --> 00:21:25.970
But when we were dealing
with the culture change,

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:28.580
and there was an ops
team on we as an SRE team

00:21:28.580 --> 00:21:31.110
we're working on the
engineering team,

00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:33.260
they often looked at
us as ops engineers

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:35.690
who could do this one
off scripting for them.

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:38.310
But that's not what an SRE is.

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:41.440
We clearly defined that
in our engagement model

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:45.230
and put it in front of
our engineering teams.

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:51.440
And our model is based
off of shared goals.

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:53.600
If you read this
SRE workbook there's

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:55.400
a whole chapter on
how you should engage

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:57.560
with your engineering
teams, and I've

00:21:57.560 --> 00:22:02.280
contributed the model
in detail on chapter 18.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:05.270
But at a high level, it's
basis of shared goals.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.760
As a team that is building
operational maturity

00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:16.490
on other teams, my
goal is to build

00:22:16.490 --> 00:22:20.030
something that can be
applied across the arc,

00:22:20.030 --> 00:22:21.260
not just with them.

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:26.120
So we use our goals, and we
use engineering teams goals,

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:32.057
align them and call that
the scope of an engagement.

00:22:32.057 --> 00:22:33.890
Once the shared goals
are set, the engineers

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:37.520
will sprint on the
milestones, and the managers

00:22:37.520 --> 00:22:41.400
will keep the
communication and feedback

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:45.120
loop alive until the end
of the communication.

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:50.340
And we measure the impact by
performing operational maturity

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:52.740
assessments, appoint
them estimates

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:56.910
of operational maturity
before we begin the engagement

00:22:56.910 --> 00:22:59.100
and after the
engagement is done.

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:03.770
And we derive the
metrics to compare

00:23:03.770 --> 00:23:06.350
what the engagement results
are, and we publish them.

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:12.290
I'll walk you through
one of the examples.

00:23:12.290 --> 00:23:15.650
As you've seen we
had this challenge

00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:17.930
of having shared
views of reliability

00:23:17.930 --> 00:23:20.660
across multiple teams.

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:25.880
So that we could capture
the user experience

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:30.740
and monitor our asset loss,
schedule a view of reliability

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:33.790
and Milestones 0.

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:35.640
We call it Milestone
0 because at the time

00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:38.130
we did not really know what
the shared view of reliability

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:39.810
meant.

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:44.580
And how we would be build
that tooling that could

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:47.430
be applied across our arc.

00:23:47.430 --> 00:23:51.330
So Milestone 0 is to
derive that requirements

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:53.490
and plan for the rollout.

00:23:53.490 --> 00:23:56.880
This is a unique engagement
because what we did was

00:23:56.880 --> 00:24:01.440
we approached our Google
TAM, and customer engineers

00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:03.780
and put out our problems.

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:06.100
And they referred
to Google's SREs.

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:12.300
So we worked alongside
of Google SREs.

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:15.770
They were, like,
partners with our team.

00:24:18.420 --> 00:24:21.960
They were participating
in regular check ins

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:24.990
to review what
SLOs really meant.

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:28.470
While we were working
with a frameworks team

00:24:28.470 --> 00:24:32.430
that had multiple apps
built on that framework.

00:24:32.430 --> 00:24:38.000
And built a tool that
could capture these SLOs

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:39.190
in a single pane of glass.

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:45.710
I'm not able to show the
exact framework SLOs here,

00:24:45.710 --> 00:24:50.960
but this is our drone SLOs
built on the same tool.

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:53.600
But it's a place
where anyone can

00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:59.444
see SLOs for all the
apps for that framework.

00:25:02.290 --> 00:25:07.020
This is an example of how
you could involve Google SREs

00:25:07.020 --> 00:25:16.690
alongside and get the benefit
of them to drive your goal.

00:25:16.690 --> 00:25:22.240
Google SREs even did a workshop
for non-technical participants

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:25.300
in that engagement, and they
explained what SLOs really

00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:28.330
meant, because it's really hard
to get SLOs right if you're

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:30.000
doing it for the first time.

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.680
And that was our first
time ever to have

00:25:32.680 --> 00:25:34.720
standard SLOs rolled out.

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:36.820
Today we have this
tool, and we are

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:38.050
working on rolling that out.

00:25:40.560 --> 00:25:43.040
So our engagements are not
just like tooling apps,

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:45.110
building operational maturity.

00:25:45.110 --> 00:25:50.330
We also work, drive, special
events at the New York Times.

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:53.712
And Vinessa will talk about
one such special event.

00:25:53.712 --> 00:25:54.545
VINESSA WAN: Thanks.

00:25:59.050 --> 00:26:03.850
This is such an exciting time
to be at the New York Times.

00:26:03.850 --> 00:26:05.530
Its events and
elections like this

00:26:05.530 --> 00:26:09.490
that make me particularly proud
to be part of the organization.

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:12.470
And elections are
always special events.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:14.950
But this year it's
been kind of different.

00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:19.530
The political climate itself,
plus the attention to our site,

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:21.590
it's just been unprecedented.

00:26:21.590 --> 00:26:26.150
And also since 2016 our digital
subscriptions have doubled.

00:26:26.150 --> 00:26:29.260
We were expecting traffic for
a midterm like never before.

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:35.070
We formed a
cross-functional project,

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:38.100
and the mission was that
New York Times would remain

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:41.820
to be a key destination
for reliable, up to date,

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:43.990
election results.

00:26:43.990 --> 00:26:46.110
It was a cross team effort.

00:26:46.110 --> 00:26:49.830
And we wanted to ensure that
our news and interactive content

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:54.360
was stable, performant,
always up to date,

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:57.930
as our newsroom intends
to for our users.

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:00.030
And we also aimed
to minimize issues

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:01.560
in case they did come up.

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:04.650
And that we would also have a
minimum response and measures

00:27:04.650 --> 00:27:08.280
in place to resolve
them quickly.

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:10.850
And I want you to remember,
this was our first ever event

00:27:10.850 --> 00:27:13.310
like this on GCP.

00:27:13.310 --> 00:27:16.700
Usually the scope for
elections typically

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:19.310
focuses on our interactive
and our publishing

00:27:19.310 --> 00:27:21.050
content up through
what kind of call

00:27:21.050 --> 00:27:25.580
the core stack of how it looks
to the end user on the site.

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:30.260
But, traditionally, we always
just take down the paywall.

00:27:30.260 --> 00:27:33.410
Because it was just too
complex and just too costly

00:27:33.410 --> 00:27:35.780
be able to do that for an event.

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:40.190
But now being able to
leverage GKE's auto

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:42.290
scaling and the
team's investment

00:27:42.290 --> 00:27:45.050
in SRE as a discipline as
well as all the work they'd

00:27:45.050 --> 00:27:47.000
done with test
automation, it let

00:27:47.000 --> 00:27:49.710
them have better basics
in place with regards

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:52.520
to performance metrics.

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:57.830
Essentially, for the first time
our business and marketing team

00:27:57.830 --> 00:28:01.940
was able to take advantage
of this surge in traffic

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:04.340
as a business opportunity.

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:06.440
We decided to leave
the paywall up

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:10.550
as a tactic to gain user
registrations on our site

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:13.163
so that later we could
reach out to those users

00:28:13.163 --> 00:28:15.080
to see if they would be
interested in becoming

00:28:15.080 --> 00:28:17.410
subscribers.

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:18.730
So this increased the scope.

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:21.640
And we were looking
at over 30 teams

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:26.110
that we needed to
assemble around readiness.

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:28.990
We were focused very much on
the technical readiness of it,

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:30.610
but it also did
involve coordination

00:28:30.610 --> 00:28:34.630
with newsroom, marketing,
business, advertising.

00:28:34.630 --> 00:28:36.220
So how did we
assemble this effort?

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:43.090
I remember being at Next
about a couple of years ago.

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:45.180
And there's a
really great talk--

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:48.090
I think GCP podcasts also
does an episode on it.

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:51.000
And it's Home Depot
with Google's SRE team,

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:55.260
and they're talking about the
preparation for Home Depot's

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:56.970
first Black Friday on GCP.

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:02.960
Both companies
partnered together.

00:29:02.960 --> 00:29:05.780
They made an upfront
commitment to roll out

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:08.680
tooling and best practices.

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:11.630
And that Google would be in
the room with the Home Depot

00:29:11.630 --> 00:29:15.040
all throughout Black Friday.

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:19.180
Home Depot's success
was Google's success.

00:29:19.180 --> 00:29:21.850
Any failure on Home
Depot's site would also

00:29:21.850 --> 00:29:24.700
be shared with Google.

00:29:24.700 --> 00:29:26.880
It was a shared fate.

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:28.880
And so when I was asked
to be one of the project

00:29:28.880 --> 00:29:31.970
managers, our project owner
being outside delivery

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:37.070
engineering, as well as a lot of
the other election leads on it,

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:40.710
we were wondering
how do we start?

00:29:40.710 --> 00:29:41.990
What kind of work do we do?

00:29:41.990 --> 00:29:46.110
And I immediately thought back
to that shared fate example.

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:50.120
We were able to partner with our
delivery engineering SRE team

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:53.870
to collaborate with them and
start mapping out the work

00:29:53.870 --> 00:29:58.880
that we needed to get all these
teams ready for elections.

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:00.560
This work started
with doing a series

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:02.690
of architectural reviews.

00:30:02.690 --> 00:30:05.240
We pooled representatives
from all these teams

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:10.520
in one big room, and they
went over their architecture

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:11.030
questions.

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:13.760
Like what fail-overs
did they have in place?

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:16.550
And our SRE team was able
to identify quick resiliency

00:30:16.550 --> 00:30:20.330
wins that they could work on.

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:23.500
Prashanth mentioned a
lot of our challenges

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:27.270
with incident management because
teams were still reliant on us

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:29.740
a central system's
infrastructure team

00:30:29.740 --> 00:30:32.080
to handle a lot of
their P1 incidents.

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:35.290
That just wouldn't
work during midterms.

00:30:35.290 --> 00:30:38.470
Our team, as prep, had
started building up

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:40.795
training materials around
incident management

00:30:40.795 --> 00:30:43.000
and what to do.

00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:45.440
How people should actually
be managing an incident.

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:47.170
These are practices
actually inspired

00:30:47.170 --> 00:30:49.630
by emergency response today.

00:30:49.630 --> 00:30:53.080
And we train over 100 people
in the technology and newsroom

00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:55.780
how to respond in case there
was a production incident, what

00:30:55.780 --> 00:30:59.230
we also refer to as a P1.

00:30:59.230 --> 00:31:01.720
And of course we partnered
with our Google TAMs.

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:03.700
They let us know on
day one of this project

00:31:03.700 --> 00:31:05.560
that they would be
with us on site,

00:31:05.560 --> 00:31:07.360
and we figured
out and documented

00:31:07.360 --> 00:31:10.750
what are all the processes
that we needed to do,

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:12.980
and also what resources
would be available.

00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:17.400
So if something happened,
they would be there for us.

00:31:17.400 --> 00:31:19.140
The rapid duration
of the project,

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:21.490
we conducted stress test.

00:31:21.490 --> 00:31:23.220
So increasing load
of the systems

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:27.340
to find out where the
vulnerabilities were.

00:31:27.340 --> 00:31:29.260
Not only did this
help us technically,

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:33.120
but after each test we would
conduct the learning review

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:34.660
or blameless postmortem.

00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:36.370
And we would do go
through the timeline

00:31:36.370 --> 00:31:38.107
and then discuss,
where did we get lucky?

00:31:38.107 --> 00:31:39.190
Where did things go right?

00:31:39.190 --> 00:31:40.820
Where did things not go wrong?

00:31:40.820 --> 00:31:43.520
And we didn't just talk about
it from a technical level.

00:31:43.520 --> 00:31:47.030
This also helped
guide logistics.

00:31:47.030 --> 00:31:49.270
Which teams had to sit
next to each other?

00:31:49.270 --> 00:31:52.210
Because you can't put
30 teams in a war room.

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:54.940
But that also just gauged
what was the optimum way

00:31:54.940 --> 00:31:58.000
that we needed to work and
communicate to make sure we

00:31:58.000 --> 00:31:59.305
were successful on midterms.

00:32:02.178 --> 00:32:03.470
So here is us during elections.

00:32:03.470 --> 00:32:06.540
See if you can find-- can
anyone find Prashanth?

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:07.040
There he is.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:07.552
Hi.

00:32:07.552 --> 00:32:09.010
And he's there with
our Google rep,

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:12.020
and we're talking with
our interactive news team.

00:32:12.020 --> 00:32:14.990
I could show more pictures, but
let's go through the results.

00:32:14.990 --> 00:32:17.350
Now, I can't go through
particular details

00:32:17.350 --> 00:32:19.120
with traffic and subscription.

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:23.860
But, yes, it was record
traffic for midterms.

00:32:23.860 --> 00:32:25.660
You can see
particularly, one zoom

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:28.650
shot, is the
registration paywall.

00:32:28.650 --> 00:32:29.800
You could before.

00:32:29.800 --> 00:32:33.000
Can anyone guess the peak
when midterms happened?

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:34.155
About 40 times its traffic.

00:32:34.155 --> 00:32:35.530
And then you could
even follow up

00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:39.100
with a news bump in
the morning news cycle.

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:44.420
This allowed significant
registration growth.

00:32:44.420 --> 00:32:46.975
And of course
incidents occurred.

00:32:46.975 --> 00:32:48.350
We did have
production incidents,

00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:50.360
but it was actually
really small.

00:32:50.360 --> 00:32:52.700
And because we had put
all this preparation

00:32:52.700 --> 00:32:57.570
into having responses in place
and fail-over plans in place.

00:32:57.570 --> 00:33:00.440
We had very minimal user impact.

00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:02.900
The resolution was
rapid and almost instant

00:33:02.900 --> 00:33:06.600
in terms of the end
user experience.

00:33:09.210 --> 00:33:12.690
So it's important to
celebrate events like this.

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:15.200
We talked about our mindset.

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:17.040
We talked about how
we work internally,

00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:19.460
how we partner
engagement with teams.

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:21.530
But how do we
evangelize ourselves

00:33:21.530 --> 00:33:24.750
across our organization?

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:27.210
I've worked on a lot of front
end teams and in advertising,

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:29.847
and it's kind of straightforward
to explain your success.

00:33:29.847 --> 00:33:31.680
You could just point
to a front end feature.

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:34.790
You could point to
a revenue impact.

00:33:34.790 --> 00:33:38.550
But our type of work,
it's hard to explain

00:33:38.550 --> 00:33:41.760
to people in the business
that don't do what we do.

00:33:41.760 --> 00:33:44.420
I often say we're kind
of like a stage crew.

00:33:44.420 --> 00:33:48.150
You know, you don't realize our
success until things actually

00:33:48.150 --> 00:33:50.970
break, and then it's-- yeah.

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:54.450
So we were also, though,
very inspired from feedback

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:57.030
that we got through
earlier feedback surveys.

00:33:57.030 --> 00:34:00.435
And someone specifically
said, I do all this work

00:34:00.435 --> 00:34:01.060
with your team.

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:02.820
And this has helped
me, but we don't really

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:04.140
get to talk about it this much.

00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:06.690
We should share more
of these stories.

00:34:06.690 --> 00:34:08.699
So inspired with that,
we decided to kick off

00:34:08.699 --> 00:34:10.980
a project within our team.

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:16.469
We held New York Times first
ever internal tech conference.

00:34:16.469 --> 00:34:18.280
Now, delivery
engineering internally

00:34:18.280 --> 00:34:20.380
we abbreviate
ourselves to DV, and we

00:34:20.380 --> 00:34:22.630
thought we'd do a fun little
word play on DevOps days,

00:34:22.630 --> 00:34:24.909
so we did DV ops day.

00:34:24.909 --> 00:34:27.760
Our CFP called out.

00:34:27.760 --> 00:34:30.010
We want to know
what's working, what's

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:34.389
not working, even since
our cloud migrations in Q2?

00:34:34.389 --> 00:34:36.909
And we wanted teams to
share their best practices

00:34:36.909 --> 00:34:39.760
for operational support,
testing, tooling

00:34:39.760 --> 00:34:41.400
and automation.

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:44.469
It was very important that we
reached out to the teams first.

00:34:44.469 --> 00:34:47.230
Because, again, we want
to empower other teams.

00:34:47.230 --> 00:34:49.510
We didn't want to
just be us talking.

00:34:49.510 --> 00:34:50.230
And it wasn't.

00:34:50.230 --> 00:34:53.320
We had 13 talks that included
presentation and demos

00:34:53.320 --> 00:34:57.460
with topics like production
deployments with App Engine,

00:34:57.460 --> 00:35:01.600
using HashiCorp Vault for
GCP service list solutions.

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:04.450
75% of these speakers
were outside of our team

00:35:04.450 --> 00:35:06.820
and most of these speakers
were first time speakers

00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:10.120
or it was a really long
time before they spoke.

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:13.390
We also had, in addition to
presentations and topics,

00:35:13.390 --> 00:35:16.570
we had a workshop on how to
facilitate learning reviews.

00:35:16.570 --> 00:35:19.360
And we also held
a panel comprised

00:35:19.360 --> 00:35:22.300
of teams that went through
production readiness reviews

00:35:22.300 --> 00:35:24.430
to talk about what
their experiences were

00:35:24.430 --> 00:35:28.280
like in working with our team.

00:35:28.280 --> 00:35:30.290
The majority of
volunteers that day

00:35:30.290 --> 00:35:31.790
were also outside
our team, which

00:35:31.790 --> 00:35:34.280
was also very impactful
to kind of just

00:35:34.280 --> 00:35:36.710
get even anybody involved.

00:35:36.710 --> 00:35:39.020
And it was a pretty snowy day.

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:42.270
We held the conference
on late January.

00:35:42.270 --> 00:35:45.080
And if anyone's in from
New York you know it snows

00:35:45.080 --> 00:35:47.220
and the commuting
is not that great.

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:50.540
But we actually had over
100 people sign up that day.

00:35:50.540 --> 00:35:53.150
And that count doesn't
include all the people

00:35:53.150 --> 00:35:54.230
who joined remotely.

00:35:57.060 --> 00:35:58.722
One snippet from our panel.

00:35:58.722 --> 00:36:00.930
This was from an audience
member who was specifically

00:36:00.930 --> 00:36:05.250
talking about the panel
production readiness reviews.

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:07.880
How powerful it was.

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:10.080
And I think the other
takeaway from this

00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:15.300
is that this person referred
to it of, wow your customers.

00:36:15.300 --> 00:36:18.620
For us that was
already a big win.

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:20.362
So going forward
we're aiming to have

00:36:20.362 --> 00:36:21.570
more of these events in tech.

00:36:21.570 --> 00:36:24.122
If anything the feedback
was overwhelmingly,

00:36:24.122 --> 00:36:25.330
we want more of these events.

00:36:25.330 --> 00:36:27.528
We want to share more
of these stories.

00:36:27.528 --> 00:36:29.070
I remember even
remembering Prashanth

00:36:29.070 --> 00:36:30.970
and I on a elevator
ride down when

00:36:30.970 --> 00:36:32.823
we were working on
this presentation,

00:36:32.823 --> 00:36:34.990
there's a developer who ran
into us in the elevator.

00:36:34.990 --> 00:36:40.928
He goes my goal next year is to
present at the next DV ops day.

00:36:40.928 --> 00:36:42.470
So I'm going to hand
off to Prashanth

00:36:42.470 --> 00:36:43.990
now, who's going to
talk more about how

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:44.907
we're looking forward.

00:36:47.792 --> 00:36:49.250
PRASHANTH SANAGAVARAPU:
By no means

00:36:49.250 --> 00:36:51.640
we have covered
all the challenges

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:58.410
that we have on the work that
we solved post migration.

00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:04.495
Looking at 2019 and beyond, we--

00:37:04.495 --> 00:37:05.820
sorry, it looks like--

00:37:13.035 --> 00:37:15.620
There's a slide missing, but--

00:37:15.620 --> 00:37:18.200
we're looking to build
operational maturity

00:37:18.200 --> 00:37:18.770
on the teams.

00:37:18.770 --> 00:37:23.570
Continue doing our engagement
with the teams to build tooling

00:37:23.570 --> 00:37:26.660
and build stability
into our applications.

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:31.010
But the quick summary,
tools can reduce toil,

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:35.990
but SREs can impact by
partnering with teams

00:37:35.990 --> 00:37:37.980
and building
operational maturity.

00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:40.790
So, think about
engaging on teams

00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:43.580
that are having specific
problems that could

00:37:43.580 --> 00:37:51.150
be replicated elsewhere easily.

00:37:51.150 --> 00:37:53.860
So you want to tackle those
through your engagements

00:37:53.860 --> 00:37:55.422
and apply product
management mindset

00:37:55.422 --> 00:37:56.880
to build that
operational majority,

00:37:56.880 --> 00:37:59.310
because building operational
maturity is a culture change.

00:37:59.310 --> 00:38:02.010
Treat your engineering
teams has your customers.

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:04.650
Ask feedback on a
regular cadence.

00:38:04.650 --> 00:38:07.980
And source that back in as your
requirements for your tooling

00:38:07.980 --> 00:38:10.410
or guidance.

00:38:10.410 --> 00:38:13.540
And define an engagement
model that is goal oriented

00:38:13.540 --> 00:38:16.080
and works for your team.

00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:19.560
Because, as I mentioned,
engaging with your teams

00:38:19.560 --> 00:38:22.410
is a key for your success.

00:38:22.410 --> 00:38:26.730
Leverage your TAMs,
customer engineers.

00:38:26.730 --> 00:38:29.590
They have insight
into what works

00:38:29.590 --> 00:38:32.460
and what doesn't, because they
have more experience tapping

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:33.900
into the resources from Google.

00:38:33.900 --> 00:38:37.830
And working with
Google SRE for us

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:43.890
has turned out into a
really great experience

00:38:43.890 --> 00:38:47.190
because we were able
to get validation

00:38:47.190 --> 00:38:48.720
from someone outside
of the company

00:38:48.720 --> 00:38:53.630
to look at the tooling and the
SLOs that we were building.

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:55.920
And evangelize
your SRE practices

00:38:55.920 --> 00:38:59.050
to stir up that culture change.

00:38:59.050 --> 00:39:01.820
Last but not least,
celebrate your success.

00:39:05.090 --> 00:39:08.970
We as a company are growing,
and my team is growing.

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:11.270
Please take a look
at our job site.

00:39:11.270 --> 00:39:14.340
We're looking for
talented engineers.

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:18.300
I know I can see a lot here.

00:39:18.300 --> 00:39:22.210
And check out our
blogs open.nytimes.com

00:39:22.210 --> 00:39:25.260
where we talk about what we do.

00:39:25.260 --> 00:39:28.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

